TI  - Medical Image Steganography Through Learning-Based Selection of Embedding Location
PY  - 2025
T2  - ACIT 2025 - 26th International Arab Conference on Information Technology, Conference Proceedings
SP  - 361
EP  - 365
DO  - 10.1109/ACIT68900.2025.11510636
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041436990?origin=resultslist
AB  - The security of medical images and patient data is a critical concern in modern healthcare, particularly with the growing reliance on telemedicine and digital data exchange. Unauthorized access or tampering can lead to serious consequences, including privacy violations, misdiagnosis, and loss of patient trust. To address these challenges, the proposed medical image steganography method is designed for the secure transmission and storage of sensitive patient data, addressing security threats prevalent in modern telemedicine. The method employs a neural network to intelligently select optimal embedding locations within a cover image, thereby minimizing visual distortion and resisting detection by steganalysis algorithms. The approach analyzes the cover image pixels, partitions them into non-overlapping blocks, and uses the neural network to identify the best regions, with a focus on high-contrast areas. The system then encrypts patient information and adaptively embeds it into the suggested highcontrast pixels using a dynamic key and hash value, ensuring high data capacity and undetectability. Additionally, a reversible extraction process is developed to accurately retrieve the hidden information by reversing the embedding procedure. This methodology significantly improves the security of medical data, making it a robust solution for modern healthcare and telemedicine applications. © 2025 IEEE.
