TI  - Mastering Concealment: Dual-Veil and the Evolution of Secure Data Transmission
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 6th International Conference on Advancements in Computational Sciences, ICACS 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICACS64902.2025.10937884
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041985753?origin=resultslist
AB  - The increasing prevalence of digital health records and the rise in data breaches necessitate robust security measures to safeguard sensitive patient information. Current methods often fail to ensure confidentiality and integrity, leaving healthcare organizations vulnerable to unauthorized access and potential legal repercussions. The proposed dual-image least significant bit (LSB) based steganography algorithm, Dual-Veil, addresses these challenges by embedding encrypted patient data within dual-channel medical images. In digital imagery, channels represent layers of visual information, evolving from simple monochromatic images to complex multi-channel compositions. The dual-channel image allows for concealing and revealing elements, merging visual components, and conveying nuanced narratives. Our approach utilizes a Stegokey-dependent function to select non-overlapping cover pixels and incorporates encryption before embedding, enhancing dynamism and improving outcomes. This methodology distinguishes between detectability and imperceptibility, concealing data similarly to LSB techniques while adhering to the Kerckhoff principle, thereby establishing new standards for digital steganographic security. Test results demonstrate enhanced algorithmic efficiency, making our contribution notably innovative compared to existing methods. Ultimately, Dual-Veil aligns with regulatory standards, such as HIPAA, and fosters trust between patients and providers by ensuring the secure transmission and storage of critical health information, significantly advancing the quest for comprehensive healthcare data protection. This methodology distinguishes between detectability and imperceptibility while establishing new standards for digital steganographic security. Test results show enhanced algorithmic efficiency, making our contribution notably innovative compared to existing methods. © 2025 IEEE.
