AU  - Tsai, S.-E.
TI  - Security-aware payload control and residual suppression for data hiding in AMBTC-compressed images
PY  - 2026
T2  - Signal Processing: Image Communication
VL  - 148
C7  - 117652
DO  - 10.1016/j.image.2026.117652
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043524640?origin=resultslist
AB  - Data hiding in Absolute Moment Block Truncation Coding (AMBTC)-compressed images is constrained by a rate-distortion-security trade-off: increasing payload often degrades either reconstruction fidelity or statistical undetectability. Existing AMBTC schemes still rely mainly on static quantization-level-difference (QLD) thresholds and fixed block rules, which cannot regulate payload precisely or adapt to heterogeneous local masking conditions. This paper presents GAN-PPO-AMBTC, a security-aware framework that couples reinforcement-learning-based payload control with adversarial quantization compensation. At the macro level, a Proximal Policy Optimization policy observes a compact joint state defined by QLD and block variance and selects one of four discrete embedding loads. A secondary Deep Q-Network dynamically rebalances payload, distortion, and steganalysis terms in the reward while a budget-aware regularization term improves consistency with the target image-level payload. At the micro level, a U-Net generator with Cross-Feedback Channels and a Double-Tanh differentiable embedding simulator jointly optimizes the bitmap and quantization pair to suppress luminance drift and residual artifacts. Experiments on BOSSbase 1.01 and BOWS-2 achieve 40.35 dB PSNR, 0.991 SSIM, and detection error rates of 46.85%, 42.54%, and 35.62% at 0.1, 0.2, and 0.4 bpp, respectively. Additional channel-robustness, ablation, and medical-image evaluations further support the effectiveness of the proposed unified allocation-and-compensation framework. The medical-image experiment is used as a structural stress test rather than as a claim of immediate clinical deployment. © 2026 The Author. Published by Elsevier B.V. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/
