TI  - Deep Learning-Based Mean Square Error (DL-MSE) in context of Computer Vision Image Compression
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 IEEE 1st International Conference on Recent Trends in Computing and Smart Mobility, RCSM 2025
DO  - 10.1109/RCSM67767.2025.11508090
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041476692?origin=resultslist
AB  - Mean Square Error (MSE) is one of the most widely used objective evaluation metrics in the field of image processing. It quantifies the average squared difference between the original image and the processed (e.g., compressed, reconstructed, or enhanced) image. The core idea is to numerically estimate how much distortion has been introduced during image manipulation. MSE becomes a cornerstone metric in image processing research due to its simplicity, computational efficiency, and ability to objectively measure distortion. While not always aligned with human perception, its role in benchmarking, model optimization, and quality evaluation is indispensable. MSE is a fundamental quality assessment tool across a wide range of image processing applications. In Image Compression, MSE is used to evaluate the degradation caused by compression algorithms. A lower MSE indicates that the compressed image retains higher fidelity to the original. In Super-Resolution and Upscaling, MSE helps quantify the accuracy of reconstructed high-resolution images against their ground truth counterparts. In de-noising applications, MSE is employed to assess how effectively noise has been removed while preserving the structural content of the original image. In Image In-painting and Restoration, It serves as a metric to evaluate how well the algorithm restores missing or damaged regions in an image. In Deep Learning and Neural Networks, MSE is often used as a loss function during training of neural networks for tasks such as image regression, enhancement, and colorization. In Steganography and Watermarking, the imperceptibility of hidden information is measured using MSE; lower values imply better concealment without visual artifacts. In medical Image analysis, especially in modalities like CT (Computed Tomography), MRI (Magnetic Resonance Imaging), or X-ray image compression, MSE is used to ensure diagnostic quality is preserved after processing. © 2025 IEEE.
