AB  - The safe keeping of multimodal medical data in the cloud is progressively at risk due to sophisticated adversarial attacks on confidentiality, integrity, and availability. This study is about a Cognitive Adversarial Attack, Recovery Architecture (CAARA) for secure multimodal medical data using deep steganographic intelligence. The system that is proposed here integrates self, learning adversarial modeling, cognitive intrusion awareness, and deep steganographic embedding for active attack detection, adaptive concealment, and fast data recovery. Multimodal inputs such as medical images, physiological signals, and clinical text are simultaneously protected using a hybrid CNN, Transformer steganographic encoder with adversarial co, evolution training. A recovery intelligence module utilizes attention, based restoration and error, correcting decoding to decode corrupted or intercepted data. Complete experiments on public medical datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of CAARA under different cyber-attack scenarios. To produce stego medical images of diagnostic quality, the proposed system attains an average PSNR of 41.82 dB and SSIM of 0.987 which are significantly high values. In the case of simulated adversarial attacks, CAARA increases the attack detection accuracy to 98.64%, and lowers the probability of data leakage by 46.3% compared to the traditional steganographic methods. Additionally, the recovery performances in terms of the reconstruction accuracy and robustness are improved by 34.7% and 29.1%, respectively, as compared to the baseline models. The results indicate that CAARA provides a resilient, intelligent, and scalable solution for safeguarding multimodal medical data in cyber healthcare infrastructures.  © 2026 IEEE.
