TI  - Dualmodal and multifunctional steganography of three-dimensional integral imaging for the Internet of Medical Things
PY  - 2026
T2  - Signal Processing
VL  - 239
C7  - 110217
DO  - 10.1016/j.sigpro.2025.110217
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105012765590?origin=resultslist
AB  - With the development of the Internet of Medical Things, precise healthcare is increasingly relying on the secure transmission and storage of medical images. Among these images, naked-eye three-dimensional (3D) images are particularly valuable because they can provide a comprehensive and continuous view of pathological regions that planar images cannot offer. In this study, a 3D dualmodal steganography of medical integral imaging is proposed, where text-fragile and image-robust watermarking are combined tailored to different 3D regions for multiple functions.After capturing an object, an elemental image remapping algorithm and deep learning are co-used to pixel-wisely extract the geometry and reflection characteristics of the scene, regulating the 3D region of interest (ROI) and non-interest (RONI). Sequentially, according to different saliency values of ROI, text data is embedded as a fragile watermark into ROI with dynamically adjusted embedding factor, enabling ROI tamper detection with pseudo-continuous naked-eye visual effect of host. By contrast, a multi-logo-multi-depth light field image is rendered as robust watermark to be embedded into the RONI, ensuring the copyright and source tracing of hospital. As a result, the dual-modal and multifunctional scheme not only detects tampering in critical diagnostic regions but also ensures data traceability under various attacks. Also, the embedded text can provide clinicians with supplementary information for more accurate treatment. Experiments are conducted to validate the imperceptibility, sensitivity and robustness of the scheme. Our solution shows promise in enhancing the accuracy and reliability of medical diagnosis, thereby strengthening the clinical potential of 3D integral imaging in secure healthcare environments. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
