TI  - InceptHide: An Inception Attention Steganography Framework for Secure Medical Image Transmission
PY  - 2026
T2  - SN Computer Science
VL  - 7
IS  - 7
C7  - 772
DO  - 10.1007/s42979-026-05354-1
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105052333406?origin=resultslist
AB  - Secure medical image transfer requires invisibility of not just the content but also the presence of the transfer itself. Existing methods for deep image steganography suffer from trade-offs between multiple features such as imperceptibility, reconstruction fidelity, robustness and computational complexity. To surmount these constraints, this work introduces InceptHide, an attention based medical steganography framework using multi-scale Inception units with channel attention in an encoder-decoder network. In InceptHide, a secret brain MRI is embedded in a cover image by the encoder and is restored by the decoder. A composite objective function, including pixel-wise loss, perceptual and structural loss, is used to balance the quality of the embedded data and the reconstruction. Evaluation of steganography shows that the PSNR and SSIM for stego images are 31.80 dB and 0.962, respectively for MRI dataset. For recovery of the secret MRI images, the PSNR and SSIM are 42.75 dB and 0.9759, respectively. Results show that InceptHide can improve the stego PSNR and SSIM by about 1.80 dB and 0.012, respectively, and the recovery PSNR by 3.26 dB all compared to the previous SE-Inception based CNN. We also validated the results on Ultrasound datasets for assessing its applicability with different structural patterns. Results also indicate that InceptHide has an appropriate balance between usability and security for secure medical image transfers. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2026.
