TI  - Gradient histogram pattern matched deep convolutive neural learning based reversible data hiding for secure communication of medical data in images
PY  - 2026
T2  - Applied Soft Computing
VL  - 201
C7  - 115718
DO  - 10.1016/j.asoc.2026.115718
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043592943?origin=resultslist
AB  - Data security plays a vital role in data sharing in telemedicine and e-health systems applications. The digitalization of data has increased the efficiency, speed, and the data vulnerable to cyber-attacks. However, to ensure the overall security of medical data (images), security measures have to be taken at each step, from the beginning, during and after the transmission of medical images. An efficient model is needed to ensure the security and integrity of the patient's diagnostic data transmitted and received through the network. A novel GRadient HIstogram Pattern Matched Deep Convolutive Neural Learning based REversible Data Hiding (GRIDEH) is developed to improve the security of communication in the healthcare system. The proposed GRIDEH method includes three different processes namely preprocessing, Data embedding, and data extraction that are implemented into deep convolutive neural learning. The deep convolutive neural learning consists of the input layer, an output layer, three hidden layers such as convolutional layer, a max pooling layer, and a fully connected layer for performing the reversible data hiding process. First, the number of brain MRI images and secret patient data are given to the input layer. The input is transferred into the convolutional layer where the preprocessing is carried out to improve image quality using a Gaussian distributive anisotropic texture filter and gradient regulated histogram predictor is applied to find the current pixel intensity for embedding. After that, the data embedding process is performed in the max polling layer by using the least weighted pattern matching revisited embedding method. With the embedding process, the stego image is obtained and sent to the receiver. Finally, the extraction process is performed at a fully connected layer to retrieve the original secret data and cover MRI brain image at the output layer. In this way, secure communication between sender and receiver is performed. Experimental evaluation is carried out using an MRI brain image dataset on factors such as run time, computational overhead, peak signal-to-noise ratio, SSIM, embedding rate and bit error rate with respect to thesizes of images. The quantitatively analyzed results demonstrate the better performance of our proposed technique when compared with the existing methods. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
